.TH AUDOGOMBLED.SH 1 "2026" "1.3.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
audogombleed.sh \- create CLIs with auto\-completable command trees
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B Create a symlink (the filename becomes the CLI name):
.RS
ln \-s ~/bin/audogombleed.sh ~/bin/\fICLINAME\fR
.RE
.PP
.B Create a config file (must match the symlink name):
.RS
~/\&.\fICLINAME\fR\&.conf
.RE
.PP
.B Source the symlink to activate tab completion:
.RS
source ~/bin/\fICLINAME\fR
.RE
.PP
.B Execute commands:
.RS
\fICLINAME\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fICOMMAND\fR \&...]
.RE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B audogombleed.sh
is a declarative framework for building shell CLIs with tab completion,
command abbreviation, help output, and command execution\&. Commands and
arguments are defined in a plain\-text config file; no coding is required\&.
.PP
The script works in both
.B bash
(4.2+) and
.BR zsh .
Runtime requirements are limited to bash (or zsh) and awk \(em both
available on every Unix system\&. There are no external dependencies to
install\&.
.PP
The config file parser is an embedded AWK script\&. It reads the config
and produces completion candidates, help text, and the command expression
to execute\&. The config file is re\-read on every tab completion and every
command execution\&.
.SH INSTALLATION
.SS Option A: Debian package
Build and install a \&.deb package:
.RS
.nf
\&./build\-deb\-sh
sudo dpkg \-i \&.\&./audogombleed_<version>_all\&.deb
.fi
.RE
For signed distribution:
.RS
.nf
\&./build\-deb\-sh \-\-sign
.fi
.RE
Requires
.B build\-essential
and
.BR debhelper .
After installation, the script is at
.BR /usr/bin/audogombleed .
.SS Option B: Homebrew (macOS)
.RS
.nf
brew tap i\-love\-coffee\-i\-love\-tea/audogombleed
brew install audogombleed
.fi
.RE
.SS Option C: Manual download
Get
.B audogombleed.sh
into a directory of your choice (e\&.g\&.
.BR ~/bin/ )\&.
.SS Create CLI symlinks
Regardless of install method, each CLI needs a symlink\&. The filename
becomes the CLI name, determines the config file path, and is used to
register tab completion:
.RS
.nf
ln \-s /usr/bin/audogombleed ~/bin/mycli    # after \&.deb install
ln \-s ~/bin/audogombleed.sh ~/bin/mycli    # after manual download
.fi
.RE
.SH GETTING STARTED
.SS Step 1: Create a symlink
The symlink filename becomes the CLI name, determines the config file
path, and is used to register tab completion:
.RS
.nf
ln \-s ~/bin/audogombleed.sh ~/bin/mycli
.fi
.RE
.PP
Using a symlink (instead of copying the script) means all your CLIs
share one source file \(em update
.B audogombleed.sh
once and every CLI picks up the new version\&.
.SS Step 2: Create a config file
Must be named \fB~/\&.\fICLINAME\fR\fB\&.conf\fR:
.RS
.nf
cat > ~/\&.mycli\&.conf <<'EOF'
[commands]
hello: echo "hello world"
EOF
.fi
.RE
.SS Step 3: Source the symlink
This registers the tab\-completion function with your shell:
.RS
.nf
source ~/bin/mycli
.fi
.RE
.SS Step 4: Try it
.RS
.nf
mycli <TAB><TAB>
mycli hello
.fi
.RE
.SS Execution modes
Without an alias, running
.I mycli some\-command
executes the script as a child process\&. Commands like
.B cd
and
.B export
have no effect on your shell\&. This is fine for external programs and is
the safe default\&.
.PP
Add an alias to make commands run in your current shell:
.RS
.nf
alias mycli='_cli_execute'
.fi
.RE
Now
.B mycli cd /some/path
actually changes your directory, and
.B mycli export FOO=bar
sets a variable in your shell\&. This also gives commands access to your
shell functions and variables, and avoids the overhead of forking a
subprocess per invocation\&.
.PP
Without alias:
.B cd
and
.B export
have no effect on your shell; shell functions and variables are not
available to commands; each invocation forks a subprocess\&.
.PP
With alias
.RB ( _cli_execute ):
.B cd
and
.B export
affect your shell; shell functions and variables are available; no
fork overhead\&.
.PP
In both modes: functions from
.B [env]
are available, argument completion works, and tab completion always
runs in the current shell\&.
.PP
Tab completion always runs in your shell regardless of mode\&.
.SS Multiple CLIs
Each symlink gets its own config file and variable namespace:
.RS
.nf
ln \-s ~/bin/audogombleed.sh ~/bin/cluster
ln \-s ~/bin/audogombleed.sh ~/bin/music
.fi
.RE
.I cluster
reads
.BR ~/\&.cluster.conf ;
.I music
reads
.BR ~/\&.music.conf .
Variables are namespaced by the CLI name, so they don't interfere with
each other\&.
.PP
If you have multiple CLIs, each
.B source
registers its own completion function\&. The last sourced CLI's completion
will be active\&. To switch, source the one you want to use:
.RS
.nf
source ~/bin/cli1    # cli1 completions active
source ~/bin/cli2    # cli2 completions active
.fi
.RE
.SH CONFIGURATION
A configuration file defines a command tree\&. Indentation and hierarchy
determine where a command resides in the tree\&.
.PP
The config file must contain a
.B [commands]
section and can optionally contain an
.B [env]
section\&.
.PP
The default config filename is
.BR ~/\&.\fICLINAME\fR\&.conf ,
where
.I CLINAME
is the symlink filename\&.
.SH "CONFIG FILE FORMAT"
.SS [commands] section
.SS Simple command
.RS
.nf
[commands]
first\-word: echo
.fi
.RE
.I first\-word
is the command name,
.I echo
is what gets executed\&.
.SS Command tree
Indentation creates hierarchy\&. The indentation width of the first
indented line sets the convention for the rest of the file:
.RS
.nf
[commands]
cd
    git\-projects
        project1: cd ~/git/project1
        project2: cd ~/git/project2
.fi
.RE
This creates commands like
.BR "cd git\-projects project1" .
.SS Command words vs\&. arguments
A command is a path through the tree\&. In
.BR "mycli deploy staging" ,
both
.I deploy
and
.I staging
are
.B command words
\(em defined by indentation in the config file\&.
.PP
Commands can also have
.B arguments
\(em values typed after the command words\&. In
.BR "mycli deploy staging v2" ,
.I v2
is an argument\&. Arguments are defined by
.B :name:type:source
lines and substituted into placeholders like
.BR \e1 .
If no arguments are defined, the command has none\&.
.PP
The last command word can also be dynamic \(em provided by a variable
.RB ( $namespaces ),
a function
.RB ( &get_namespaces ),
or a static list
.RB ( staging|production )\&.
This blurs the line: the user types what looks like an argument, but
it's actually selecting a command word\&.
.SS Comments
Lines beginning with
.B #
are comments that document the next command or tree element\&.
Display help by appending
.B ?
or
.B \-h
to a command:
.RS
.nf
$ mycli deploy ?
deployment commands
.fi
.RE
There are four levels of comments:
.TP
.B Global header
Consecutive
.B #
lines at the very top of
.BR [commands] ,
terminated by a blank line\&. Appears at the top of
.B mycli ?
output, above all section headings\&. Use for CLI name, description, or
usage hints:
.RS
.nf
[commands]
# mycli \(em manage widgets and gadgets
# Run 'mycli <command> ?' for help\&.

# deployment commands
deploy
    \&.\&.\&.
.fi
.RE
.RS
.nf
$ mycli ?
  mycli \(em manage widgets and gadgets
  Run 'mycli <command> ?' for help\&.

  deployment commands
    d[eploy] \&.\&.\&.
.fi
.RE
If the blank line is omitted, the
.B #
lines become section headings for the first group instead\&.
.TP
.B Section headings
A
.B #
comment at the top level (no indentation) before a command group\&.
Appears above the group in global help\&.
.TP
.B Command help
A
.B #
comment directly before a command (at the same indentation level)\&.
Appears inline with the command in help output:
.RS
.nf
deploy
    # deploy to production
    prod: \&./deploy\&.sh prod

$ mycli deploy ?
  d[eploy] p[rod]          deploy to production
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B Detail comments
Lines beginning with
.B ##
(double hash)\&. These provide extra detail text shown when viewing a
specific command's help, displayed below the command's
.B #
help text:
.RS
.nf
deploy
    ## requires AWS credentials in ~/\&.aws/credentials
    ## runs in us\-east\-1 by default
    # deploy to production
    prod: \&./deploy\&.sh prod

$ mycli deploy prod ?
  deploy to production
    requires AWS credentials in ~/\&.aws/credentials
    runs in us\-east\-1 by default
.fi
.RE
.B ##
comments can also be placed before a command group to provide
group\-level detail\&.
.SS Argument placeholders
.B \e0
is replaced by the last word of the command path (the word before the
colon)\&.
.BR \e1 ", " \e2 ", \&.\&.\&."
are replaced by user\-supplied arguments, matching the
.B :name:type:source
definitions in order:
.RS
.nf
[commands]
deploy: \&./deploy\&.sh \-\-env \e1 \-\-tag \e2
    :environment:list:staging|prod
    :tag:list:v1|v2|v3

$ mycli deploy staging v2
>> executes: \&./deploy\&.sh \-\-env staging \-\-tag v2
.fi
.RE
.B \e1
maps to the first argument definition
.RB ( :environment ),
.B \e2
to the second
.RB ( :tag )\&.
If not all placeholders are used, remaining arguments are appended to
the end of the command\&.
.PP
.B Note:
.B \e0
and
.B \e1+
come from different sources\&.
.B \e0
is always the last command word \(em most useful with expanded commands
.RB ( $variable ,
.BR &function ,
.BR val1|val2 )
where the expanded word carries meaning (e\&.g\&., a namespace name)\&.
.B \e1+
are the user's arguments after the command words\&.
.SS Arguments
Arguments are defined with
.B :\fIname\fR:\fItype\fR:\fIsource\fR
syntax after a command definition\&.
.SS Argument types
.PP
Arguments are defined as \fB:\fIname\fB:\fItype\fR lines below a command\&.
The following types are supported:
.TP
.B :arg:list:\fIval1\fR|\fIval2\fR|\fIval3\fR
Complete from a fixed set of values\&.
.TP
.B :arg:list:$\fIVAR\fR
Complete from a shell variable\&. The variable must be
.BR export ed
because the config parser runs as an AWK subprocess that can only see
exported environment variables\&.
.TP
.B :arg:eval:\fIfunc\fR
Complete from function output\&. The function is called at completion
time\&.
.TP
.B :arg:value:\fIdefault\fR
Use a default value (not a completion list)\&.
.TP
.B :arg:STRING
Free\-form string argument\&.
.TP
.B :arg:INTEGER
Integer value\&.
.TP
.B :arg:int_range:\fImin\fR\-\fImax\fR
Integer within a range (inclusive)\&.
.TP
.B :arg:FILE
Complete file paths\&.
.TP
.B :arg:DIR
Complete directory paths\&.
.TP
.B :arg:ENVVAR
Complete environment variable names\&.
.TP
.B :arg:USER
Complete system usernames\&.
.TP
.B :arg:GROUP
Complete system group names\&.
.TP
.B :arg:SSH_HOST
Complete from
.B ~/\&.ssh/config
host entries\&.
.TP
.B :arg:BLKDEV
Complete block device names\&.
.TP
.B :arg:SERVICE
Complete systemd service names\&.
.PP
Arguments can be made optional by appending
.B ?
to the type (e\&.g\&.,
.BR :arg:list?:val1|val2 )\&.
Optional arguments must come after all required arguments\&.
.PP
An optional description can be appended as the last field\&. In types
with a value field (list, int_range, eval) it comes after the value;
in all other types it comes directly after the type:
.RS
.nf
:name:type:description              (STRING, INTEGER, FILE, etc.)
:name:type:value:description        (list, int_range, eval)
.fi
.RE
Descriptions appear as
.B [description]
suffixes in zsh tab completions\&. They are ignored in bash\&.
.RS
.nf
[commands]
deploy: \&./deploy\&.sh \e1
    :env:list:staging|prod:target environment
    :tag:list?:v1|v2|v3:release tag
    :port:int_range:1\-65535:TCP port number
.fi
.RE
.SS [env] section
All lines in the
.B [env]
section are sourced before each completion and command execution\&.
.PP
.B Performance note:
the
.B [env]
section runs on
.B every
tab completion and
.B every
command execution\&. Avoid slow operations (network calls, heavy
computation) in
.B [env]
\(em they will block completion and add latency to every invocation\&. If
you need dynamic values for argument completion, use
.B eval
argument types instead, which run only at completion time\&.
.PP
Supported uses:
.IP \(bu 2
Set CLI configuration options
.IP \(bu 2
Define variables or functions for argument completion lists
.IP \(bu 2
Source other shell scripts
.IP \(bu 2
Include other config files with
.B include_commands_from
.PP
Example:
.RS
.nf
[env]
MY_OPTIONS="opt1 opt2 opt3"
function get_items { echo "foo"; echo "bar"; }
source ~/bin/custom\-cli\-function\&.sh
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B Warning:
the
.B [env]
section (including
.B source
directives) runs in the current shell \(em this is necessary so that tab
completion can access the defined variables and functions\&. This means
sourced files can overwrite existing shell variables or functions\&. Use
unique variable names or prefix them to avoid collisions\&.
.PP
Paths with
.B ~
are expanded to
.B $HOME
in
.B source
directives\&.
.SS Variable expansion ($)
A command word beginning with
.B $
expands to one command per word in the variable:
.RS
.nf
[env]
export NAMESPACES="default kube\-system monitoring"

[commands]
ns\-logs
    $NAMESPACES: kubectl logs \-f \-n \e0
.fi
.RE
The variable must be
.BR export ed\&.
The config parser is an embedded AWK script that runs as a subprocess \(em
AWK can only see exported environment variables, not regular shell
variables from
.BR [env] \&.
Use a
.B __CLI_
prefix to avoid polluting the environment of child processes\&.
.PP
Use
.B $
for short, static lists where a function would be overkill\&. Use
.B &func
when the list is dynamic, derived from a command, or stored in an
array\&.
.SS Function expansion (&)
A command word beginning with
.B &
expands using the output of a function:
.RS
.nf
[env]
function get_names { echo "alice bob"; }

[commands]
greet
    &get_names: echo "Hello \e0"
.fi
.RE
The function runs once during config loading\&. Each line of output
becomes a command word\&.
.PP
The difference from
.BR $ :
a variable holds a fixed string\&. A function can read files, call APIs,
compute a list dynamically, or unwrap a bash/zsh array (which can't be
exported)\&. The function's output is captured at load time \(em it is not
re\-evaluated on each invocation or completion\&.
.PP
Arrays are a common use case:
.RS
.nf
[env]
NAMESPACES=(default kube\-system monitoring)

function namespaces_from_array() {
    printf '%s\en' "${NAMESPACES[@]}"
}

[commands]
ns\-logs
    &namespaces_from_array: kubectl logs \-f \-n \e0
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B Note:
.B &
expands command words\&. To complete
.I arguments
from a function, use
.B :argname:eval:function_name
instead\&.
.SS List expansion (|)
A static inline list\&. Simplest option for a small, fixed set of words:
.RS
.nf
[commands]
ctx
    prod|staging|dev: kubectl config use\-context \e0
.fi
.RE
This is the command\-word equivalent of
.B :arg:list:val1|val2|val3
for arguments\&. Unlike
.BR $ ,
no export or shell interaction is needed \(em the list is parsed directly
from the config text\&. Use
.B |
for small, fixed lists that only appear in one command\&. If the same list
is shared across multiple commands, use
.B $
or
.B &
to avoid repeating it\&.
.SS Combining expansion with argument completion
All three expansion types work with argument placeholders and argument
completion\&. Variable expansion for namespaces,
.B eval
for dynamic argument lists:
.RS
.nf
[env]
export NAMESPACES="default kube\-system monitoring"

function get_deployments() {
    kubectl get deployments \-n "$1" \-o \e
        jsonpath='{\&.items[*]\&.metadata\&.name}' | tr ' ' '\en'
}

[commands]
restart
    $NAMESPACES: kubectl rollout restart deployment/\e1 \-n \e0
        :deployment:eval:get_deployments \e0
.fi
.RE
.SS Hierarchical configuration
Split command trees across multiple files with
.B include_commands_from
in the
.B [env]
section:
.RS
.nf
[env]
include_commands_from ~/\&.cli\-module\&.conf parent\-word
.fi
.RE
The included file's
.B [commands]
section is merged under
.I parent\-word
in the main config\&. Use
.B ROOT
as the parent to merge at the top level:
.RS
.nf
include_commands_from ~/\&.cli\-module\&.conf ROOT
.fi
.RE
Each included file's commands are indented under the parent command
word\&. With
.BR ROOT ,
they are merged without a parent wrapper\&.
.PP
.B Error handling:
.IP \(bu 2
.B Missing file:
if the included config file does not exist, the CLI prints an error to
stderr and stops\&.
.IP \(bu 2
.B No [commands] section:
if the included file exists but has no
.B [commands]
section, the include is silently ignored\&.
.IP \(bu 2
.B One level only:
.B include_commands_from
only works in the main config's
.B [env]
section\&. Included files cannot include further files\&. Any
.B include_commands_from
in an included file is treated as a shell command and executed, not
parsed as an include directive\&.
.PP
Included files are subject to the same file permission checks as the
main config file (see
.B SECURITY
below)\&.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-batch
Run in script/batch mode\&. Disables all output on stdout and stderr and
features that require interactive input\&. The CLI argument takes
precedence over config file settings\&. Equivalent to setting:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B __CLI_CFG_EXEC_SILENT="y"
.IP \(bu 2
.B __CLI_CFG_EXEC_EXPAND_ABBREVIATED_COMMANDS="n"
.IP \(bu 2
.B __CLI_CFG_EXEC_EXPAND_ABBREVIATED_ARGS="n"
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the script version and exit\&.
.TP
.B ?\fR, \fB\-h\fR, \fB\-?\fR, \fB\?
Display help for the current command or all available commands\&.
.PP
The following options are for development and debugging only:
.TP
.B \-\-cli\-print\-awk\-script
Print the embedded AWK config parser script\&.
.TP
.B \-\-cli\-print\-env
Print the parsed
.B [env]
section\&.
.TP
.B \-\-cli\-run\-awk\-command \fIARGS\fR
Run the embedded AWK config parser directly with the given arguments\&.
.SH "CONFIGURATION OPTIONS"
Set these in the
.B [env]
section of the config file\&.
All option names are prefixed with
.B __CLI_CFG_
in the config file\&.
.TP
.B __CLI_CFG_LOG_LEVEL
Default: 0\&. 0 means off, 4 means debug\&. When set to 4, a log file is
created under
.B /tmp
(filename uses
.BR mktemp ,
so the exact name varies \(em check
.B ls /tmp/cli\-*
to find it)\&. Debug output slows the CLI noticeably\&.
.TP
.B __CLI_CFG_EXEC_EXPAND_ABBREVIATED_COMMANDS
Default: "y"\&. Allow abbreviated commands\&. For example, with commands
.I docker list containers
and
.IR "docker list images" ,
typing
.B d l c
expands to
.B docker list containers
\(em as long as the abbreviation is unambiguous\&.
.TP
.B __CLI_CFG_EXEC_EXPAND_ABBREVIATED_ARGS
Default: "n"\&. Allow abbreviated argument values\&. Disabled by default
because it can be risky \(em you should know exactly what is happening\&.
.TP
.B __CLI_CFG_EXEC_ACK_EXPANDED_COMMANDS
Default: "y"\&. When command expansion changes the submitted command, the
user is asked to confirm before execution\&.
.TP
.B __CLI_CFG_EXEC_PRINT_HELP_ON_INCOMPLETE_ARGS
Default: "y"\&. Print help output for the command if not all required
arguments were supplied\&.
.TP
.B __CLI_CFG_EXEC_ARGS_ALLOW_COMPLETION_RESULTS_ONLY
Default: "n"\&. Only allow values from the auto\-complete list\&. By default,
even if an argument has a completion list, arbitrary values are accepted\&.
.TP
.B __CLI_CFG_EXEC_ALWAYS_RETURN_0
Default: "n"\&. Always return exit code 0\&. Useful on Ubuntu where bash is
configured to only keep succeeding commands in history\&.
.TP
.B __CLI_CFG_EXEC_SILENT
Default: "n"\&. Suppress all CLI output on stdout and stderr\&. When set to
.BR "y" ,
the following are also overridden:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B __CLI_CFG_EXEC_EXPAND_ABBREVIATED_COMMANDS="n"
.IP \(bu 2
.B __CLI_CFG_EXEC_EXPAND_ABBREVIATED_ARGS="n"
.RE
This is because abbreviated command expansion normally asks the user to
confirm before execution \(em that prompt is impossible when output is
suppressed\&. The same overrides are applied by
.B \-b
/
.BR \-\-batch \&.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 49
Script called with wrong name \(em create a symlink\&.
.TP
.B 50
No command supplied\&.
.TP
.B 51
Abbreviated command expansion failed (command not recognized or
ambiguous)\&.
.TP
.B 52
Not all positional arguments could be resolved (more placeholders than
arguments provided)\&.
.TP
.B 53
Not enough arguments provided (command has required arguments but none
were supplied)\&.
.TP
.B 127
Command not found by the shell\&.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Simple CLI
.RS
.nf
[commands]
hello: echo "hello world"
.fi
.RE
.SS Kubernetes admin CLI
.RS
.nf
[env]
function get_deployments() {
    kubectl get deployments \-o \e
        jsonpath='{\&.items[*]\&.metadata\&.name}' | tr ' ' '\en'
}

[commands]
pods
    restart: kubectl rollout restart deployment/\e1
        :deployment:eval:get_deployments
    logs: kubectl logs \-f deployment/\e1 \-\-tail=50
        :deployment:eval:get_deployments
    shell: kubectl exec \-it deployment/\e1 \-\- /bin/sh
        :deployment:eval:get_deployments
.fi
.RE
.SS Kubernetes admin with variable expansion
.RS
.nf
[env]
export NAMESPACES="default kube\-system monitoring"

function get_pods() {
    kubectl get pods \-n "$1" \-o \e
        jsonpath='{\&.items[*]\&.metadata\&.name}' | tr ' ' '\en'
}

[commands]
logs
    $NAMESPACES: kubectl logs \-f \-n \e0 \e1
        :pod:eval:get_pods \e0
.fi
.RE
.SS Terraform wrapper
.RS
.nf
[commands]
plan
    staging: terraform plan \-var\-file=staging\&.tfvars
    prod: terraform plan \-var\-file=prod\&.tfvars
apply
    staging: terraform apply \-var\-file=staging\&.tfvars
    prod: terraform apply \-var\-file=prod\&.tfvars
.fi
.RE
.SS Abbreviated commands
With the above config, abbreviated commands work:
.RS
.nf
$ tf p s
>> expands to: terraform plan \-var\-file=staging\&.tfvars
.fi
.RE
.SS Help output with global header and sections
.RS
.nf
[commands]
# My Infrastructure CLI
# Run 'mycli <command> ?' for help\&.

# deployment commands
deploy
    # deploy to production
    prod: \&./deploy\&.sh prod
    # deploy to staging
    staging: \&./deploy\&.sh staging

# utility commands
version: echo "1.0.0"

$ mycli ?
  My Infrastructure CLI
  Run 'mycli <command> ?' for help\&.

  deployment commands
    d[eploy] p[rod]          deploy to production
    d[eploy] s[taging]       deploy to staging

  utility commands
    v[ersion]
.fi
.RE
.SH SECURITY
.SS Trust model
The config file is shell code\&. Anyone who can write to
.B ~/\&.\fICLINAME\fR\&.conf
can execute arbitrary commands in your shell\&. This includes:
.IP \(bu 2
The
.B [env]
section \(em sourced directly into your shell on every completion
.IP \(bu 2
The
.B [commands]
section \(em command expressions are passed to
.B eval
.IP \(bu 2
.B source
directives \(em load external files into your shell
.IP \(bu 2
.B include_commands_from
\(em merge external config files into the command tree
.IP \(bu 2
.B :arg:eval:function
argument types \(em call functions during tab completion
.PP
The config file has the same privilege level as your
.BR \&.bashrc \&.
Treat it accordingly\&.
.SS Why eval
The core execution mechanism is:
.RS
.nf
eval $cmd_expr ${args[*]}
.fi
.RE
Where
.I cmd_expr
comes from the config file and
.I args
are the user's arguments\&. This is a deliberate design choice: a
declarative config file that maps command names to shell expressions
needs a way to execute those expressions\&. In a POSIX shell,
.B eval
is that way\&. There is no safe subset of
.B eval
\(em it interprets the full shell grammar, including command
substitution, pipelines, and redirections\&.
.SS File permission checks
The tool checks files before sourcing them\&. A file is rejected if:
.IP \(bu 2
Not a regular file (prevents sourcing device files or named pipes)
.IP \(bu 2
World\-writable (prevents sourcing files any user can modify)
.IP \(bu 2
Not owned by current user or root (prevents sourcing files planted by
other users)
.IP \(bu 2
Symlink target is not a regular file or is world\-writable
.PP
These checks apply to the main config file, files referenced by
.B source
directives, and files referenced by
.BR include_commands_from \&.
.PP
.B What these checks do NOT protect against:
a config file owned by you that was modified by an attacker who already
has your credentials, a config file in a directory writable by other
users, config content that is syntactically valid but semantically
malicious, or arguments containing shell metacharacters\&.
.SS Recommendations
.IP "1." 4
Set restrictive permissions on your config file:
.B chmod 600 ~/\&.\fICLINAME\fR\&.conf
.IP "2." 4
Don't use
.B eval
\-type arguments with untrusted input\&.
.IP "3." 4
Use
.B \-\-batch
/
.B \-b
in scripts\&.
.IP "4." 4
Review your config file as executable code\&.
.IP "5." 4
Don't put secrets in the
.B [env]
section\&.
.IP "6." 4
.B Don't use audogombleed\&.sh on shared systems
unless you control all users\&.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/\&.\fICLINAME\fR\&.conf
Default configuration file\&. The filename must match the symlink name\&.
Subject to file permission checks (see
.BR SECURITY )\&.
.TP
.I /tmp/cli\-XXXXXX\-bash\&.log
Debug log file (bash)\&. Created when
.B __CLI_CFG_LOG_LEVEL
is set to 4\&. The
.I XXXXXX
portion is random (generated by
.BR mktemp )\&.
.TP
.I /tmp/cli\-XXXXXX\-zsh\&.log
Debug log file (zsh)\&. Created when
.B __CLI_CFG_LOG_LEVEL
is set to 4\&.
.SH "SHELL COMPATIBILITY"
.SS Supported shells
.IP \(bu 2
.B Linux:
full native support\&. Tested with bash 4\&.2\-5\&.3 and zsh\&.
.IP \(bu 2
.B macOS:
native support\&. macOS ships zsh as default, which is fully supported\&.
Bash 4\&.2+ is also supported (install via
.BR "brew install bash" )\&.
The system bash (3\&.2) is
.B not
supported\&.
.IP \(bu 2
.B Windows:
supported via WSL (Windows Subsystem for Linux)\&. Git Bash / MSYS2 are
not officially supported\&.
.SS Known limitations
.IP \(bu 2
.B zsh \-c sourcing:
when the CLI is invoked via
.BR "zsh \-c 'source \&./cli; \&.\&.\&. '" ,
errors may exit the calling shell instead of returning\&.
Workaround: use the alias or invoke the CLI directly\&.
.IP \(bu 2
.B Argument completion in zsh:
some argument types (FILE, DIR, ENVVAR, USER, GROUP, SSH_HOST, BLKDEV,
SERVICE) may not complete as expected in zsh\&. These work fully in
bash\&.
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
.SS Why doesn't the command help print anything?
Because of shell globbing, the
.B ?
at the end of the line is replaced by matching filenames if there are
single\-character files in your working directory\&.
.PP
Solutions:
.IP \(bu 2
Change to a directory without single\-character files
.IP \(bu 2
Escape the
.B ?
with a backslash:
.B mycli \e?
.IP \(bu 2
Use
.B \-h
or
.B \-?
instead:
.B mycli \-h
.SS Why doesn't tab completion work?
Make sure you sourced the symlink and created the alias:
.RS
.nf
source ~/bin/mycli
alias mycli='_cli_execute'
.fi
.RE
Both lines must be in your
.B \&.bashrc
or
.BR \&.zshrc \&.
The source registers the completion function; the alias makes the
command name invoke execution in the current shell\&.
.SS Why aren't my config changes taking effect?
The config file is re\-read on every completion and execution\&. If
changes don't appear:
.IP \(bu 2
Check that the config filename matches the symlink name
.RB ( ~/\&.\fINAME\fR\&.conf )
.IP \(bu 2
Check for syntax errors \(em unclosed quotes, missing section headers
.IP \(bu 2
Set
.B __CLI_CFG_LOG_LEVEL=4
in the
.B [env]
section and check the log file
.SS Why don't zsh completions work?
Add these to your
.BR \&.zshrc :
.RS
.nf
autoload bashcompinit
bashcompinit
.fi
.RE
If you use an alias for the CLI command, also add:
.RS
.nf
setopt completealiases
.fi
.RE
.SS How do I debug my config?
Set the log level in the
.B [env]
section:
.RS
.nf
[env]
__CLI_CFG_LOG_LEVEL=4
.fi
.RE
Then check the log file:
.RS
.nf
ls /tmp/cli\-*
.fi
.RE
The log filename uses
.B mktemp
so the
.I XXXXXX
portion is random\&.
.B Note:
debug output slows the CLI down noticeably\&.
.SS How does command abbreviation work?
Commands can be submitted in abbreviated form as long as all command
words resolve unambiguously\&. For example, with commands
.I get pods
and
.IR "get services" ,
typing
.B g p
expands to
.B get pods
\(em there is no other command starting with
.B g
and
.BR p \&.
.PP
By default, the CLI asks for confirmation before executing expanded
commands\&. Use
.B \-b
/
.B \-\-batch
to disable the confirmation prompt in scripts\&.
.SH NOTES
.IP \(bu 2
The script is not intended to be called directly\&. Always create a
symlink with the desired CLI name\&.
.IP \(bu 2
Tab completion is registered on
.BR source \&.
The last sourced CLI's completion will be active\&.
.IP \(bu 2
Command abbreviation requires the abbreviation to be unambiguous among
all defined commands at that level\&.
.IP \(bu 2
The
.B [env]
section runs on every tab completion and every command execution\&. Keep
it fast\&.
.IP \(bu 2
Variables used for
.B $
command\-word expansion must be
.BR export ed\&.
.IP \(bu 2
The CLI name (symlink filename) may only contain letters, digits, and
underscores\&. Dots and dashes are rejected\&.
